 ## Abstract
 
 End-to-end argument mining identifies argumentative components and their relations directly from raw text. Dependencies between components and relations create two related problems. During training, a local paraphrase may alter the argumentative role of a component and invalidate inherited labels. During inference, changing a predicted span or type may invalidate the endpoints of associated relations. Building on prior work on conclusion-specific modeling, multi-candidate reranking, and bounded repair, this thesis studies supervision preservation during training and structural recovery during inference.
 
-The first method is relation-aware augmentation learning for conclusion components. It defines an affected relation domain from component dependencies and checks both annotated relations and no-relation states within and across the editable set. Annotation transfer establishes correspondences between original and augmented documents. Paired component and relation learning then connects accepted text variations to the shared generation representation.
+The first method combines relation-domain-constrained augmentation with paired factor learning. It defines an affected relation domain from component dependencies and checks both annotated relations and no-relation states within and across the editable set. Annotation transfer establishes correspondences between original and augmented documents. Paired component and relation learning then connects accepted text variations to the shared generation representation.
 
 The second method is dependency-closed argument graph repair driven by multiple candidates. It leaves the input text unchanged and combines compatible component-set replacement with reconstruction of all affected relations. Each proposed transaction is accepted or rolled back as a whole under complete-object scores, global structural constraints, and explicit editing budgets. Set replacement represents insertion, deletion, span or type changes, splitting, and merging. Conditional analysis characterizes local utility differences and bounded execution without guaranteeing improvement in true extraction F1.
 
-Under the AAEC and CDCP task representations, T1 improves strict relation F1 over T0 by 7.32 and 7.21 percentage points, respectively, while BR-J adds 5.99 and 6.71 points over the T1 initial predictions. Comparisons on identical augmentation sets separate the effects of auxiliary supervision and paired alignment. The sequential baseline and joint strategy differ in information use, acceptance conditions, and proposal ranking; their contrast does not isolate execution order alone. The full combination improves on the common starting point by 13.31 and 13.92 points, but the relation interaction intervals include zero. Candidate limitations, long source sets, and harmful edits remain relevant, and local repair trades quality and computation against global optimization.
-
-Keywords: End-to-End Argument Mining; Relation-Aware Augmentation; Conclusion Components; Dependency Closure; Graph Repair; Cost-Sensitive Scoring
+This edition uses artificial count scenarios to demonstrate evaluation and analysis under the native AAEC and CDCP task representations; the numbers are not empirical model results. In these scenarios, full augmentation with the base loss exceeds the QOS-style adaptation by 1.79 and 1.83 relation F1 points, while paired learning adds 1.92 and 1.88 points over head supervision on the same augmented data. The intervals for conclusion-center differences and relation interactions include zero. BR-J adds 5.99 and 6.71 points over T1 initial predictions, but equal-solver-budget global ILP attains slightly higher relation F1 and initial true-positive retention; BR-J saves only 4.19% and 4.73% of end-to-end time. These comparisons focus the first contribution on supervision correspondence and paired learning, and frame the second as a structural repair option whose value depends on resource and change-control requirements. Empirical error prevalence, object-level complementarity, and external-system comparisons require corresponding execution evidence.
+
+Keywords: End-to-End Argument Mining; Affected Relation Domain; Paired Learning; Dependency Closure; Graph Repair; Structured Prediction
 
